# Curio scheduled jobs — install with `crontab -e` as the `io` user.
#
# No long-lived scheduler process: the brief targets a Pi 3 already running
# several services, and an APScheduler thread would sit in memory all day for
# a job that runs once an hour. cron costs nothing when idle.

CURIO=/home/io/curio/backend
FLASK=/home/io/curio/backend/.venv/bin/flask

# Doors for today. Runs hourly; the command itself decides who is actually due
# based on each user's send_hour, frequency, last_sent_on, and timezone — so a
# user who picked 07:00 gets it at 07:00 on their own clock, and nobody gets
# two in a day. Users whose settings predate timezone capture fall back to
# CURIO_DEFAULT_TZ from .env.
0 * * * * cd $CURIO && $FLASK send-due-emails >> /home/io/curio/logs/email.log 2>&1

# Nightly tidy: prune rate-limit counters, old model stats, spent door tokens.
17 3 * * * cd $CURIO && $FLASK housekeeping >> /home/io/curio/logs/housekeeping.log 2>&1

# Nightly backup. SQLite's .backup is safe against a live WAL database —
# a plain `cp` of a file being written is not.
# Requires no sqlite3 CLI — the script drives the online backup API via python3.
30 3 * * * /home/io/curio/deploy/backup.sh >> /home/io/curio/logs/backup.log 2>&1

# Retune the model chain from production evidence. The free catalogue churns
# without notice — models get renamed, retired, and rate-limited, and a chain
# that worked last week can be dead or dog-slow this week.
#
# tune-chain ranks models from model_stats (the record of every real call),
# evicts anything that failed everything in the last day, and spends at most
# ~6 paced live calls probing quiet backups and a rotating slice of the
# catalogue. It never benchmarks the whole catalogue at once — that trips the
# free tier's rate limits and measures nothing. `flask refresh-chain` remains
# as a manual full-rebuild tool.
#
# Daily, at an hour nobody is reading.
40 4 * * * cd $CURIO && $FLASK tune-chain >> /home/io/curio/logs/chain.log 2>&1

# Pre-generate pages for starter doors that aren't cached yet, so a visitor's
# first tap opens instantly. Sequential, small, and at a quiet hour: the cost
# is OpenRouter free-tier calls (never user quota), and the page cache TTL
# means roughly this many need re-warming each night once fully covered.
50 4 * * * cd $CURIO && $FLASK warm-cache --limit 12 >> /home/io/curio/logs/warm.log 2>&1
